statement:
Let \(f\) be a function with the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(f(n)\) is defined for every positive integer \(n\);
\item \(f(n)\) is an integer;
\item \(f(2) = 2\);
\item \(f(mn) = f(m)f(n)\) for all \(m\) and \(n\);
\item \(f(m) > f(n)\) whenever \(m > n\).
\end{enumerate}
Prove that \(f(n) = n\).
